## Lab Grown Marquise Setting Pairing

Lab and natural marquise stones have identical physical dimensions at each carat weight — every standard marquise setting fits a lab stone perfectly. But the metal and style choice matters for maximising the visual impact of your specific stone.

**White metal for D-E colour lab stones.** Lab marquise stones graded D-E look best in platinum or white gold — the cool metal amplifies the stone's icy white face-up. A D-VVS1 2ct lab marquise in white gold at $1,200 gives you a complete ring under $3,700. The same stone in yellow gold dulls the colour advantage you paid for. See our [white gold marquise engagement ring guide](/white-gold-marquise-engagement-ring) for the full setting selection.

**Six-prong for 1ct–1.5ct lab stones.** The marquise's pointed tips are the most vulnerable points. A six-prong solitaire with two prongs at each tip is the standard protection. At 1ct–1.5ct lab, the six-prong classic solitaire (from $1,355) is the correct default — it shows the lab stone cleanly with full tip security.

**Bezel for active-lifestyle wear.** If you're buying a 2ct lab marquise for daily active wear, consider the bezel setting — it fully wraps both tips in metal. The Comfort Fit Bezel Solitaire (from $1,250) eliminates tip-chip risk entirely. The trade-off is slightly reduced brilliance from the metal lip blocking side light. See our [marquise bezel engagement ring guide](/marquise-bezel-engagement-ring) for the full tip-protection analysis.

**Three-stone settings unlock with lab economics.** At natural 2ct pricing ($32,000 for the centre alone), a three-stone setting with matched 0.5ct side stones becomes a $50,000+ ring. At lab 2ct pricing ($2,403), you can add a marquise three-stone setting from $1,620 — the complete ring stays under $4,100. Lab economics make three-stone and halo settings genuinely accessible at statement sizes.

