## Pros and Cons of Lab Grown Marquise Diamonds

**Pros:**
- **70%+ savings vs natural at every size 1ct+** — the core economic case, verified at all 4 tiers.
- **Identical carbon lattice** — same hardness (10 Mohs), same refractive index (2.42), same dispersion (0.044) as mined marquise.
- **Higher grade accessible** — D-VVS1 at 1ct for $958 vs $6,000 natural. The lab budget buys better grades.
- **GCAL 8X guarantee** — stronger grade certainty than any natural diamond certification process.
- **No conflict mineral concerns** — grown in a controlled facility with documented origin.
- **Elongation Advantage** — no scarcity premium on ideal 1.95–2.05 L:W stones. Reject freely.
- **Three-stone and halo rings become affordable** — lab economics unlock settings that natural budgets can't reach at 2ct+.

**Cons:**
- **Low resale value** — lab diamond prices have fallen 70%+ since 2021 as production scales. A $2,400 lab stone may sell for $400–$600. This is not an investment.
- **Rapidly declining prices** — good for new buyers, bad for anyone who already bought. Lab prices continue to fall.
- **Some certificates need verification** — always check the certificate number on igi.org or gcalusa.com before finalising a purchase. Misrepresentation exists at non-certified retailers.
- **Bow-tie risk unchanged** — lab or natural, a poorly cut marquise has a bow-tie shadow. IGI Ideal/Excellent is a filter, not a guarantee. Watch the 360° video. See our [marquise diamond bow-tie guide](/marquise-diamond-bow-tie).
- **Smaller secondary market** — not all jewellers buy back lab diamonds. Plan to wear the stone, not to sell it.

> **"The resale question is the honest one and I always answer it directly. A lab diamond is not an investment. A 2ct lab marquise at $2,400 today will likely sell for $400–$700 in five years. I'm transparent about that. What I'm also transparent about is this: a 2ct natural marquise at $32,000 will likely sell for $15,000–$20,000 — which is also a massive loss in absolute dollars. Percentage-wise, the losses are similar. In absolute dollars, the lab buyer loses far less. Buy the stone you love wearing, not the one you plan to sell."** — Farzana Hasan, GIA Diamond Expert

## Frequently Asked Questions

### What is a lab grown marquise diamond?

A lab grown marquise diamond is a marquise-shaped diamond grown in a reactor (CVD or HPHT process) rather than mined. The chemical formula is identical to a natural diamond: pure carbon crystal. Same hardness (10 Mohs), same brilliance, same refractive index (2.42). The only difference is origin. The FTC revised its diamond definition in 2018 to include lab-grown stones.

### Are lab grown marquise diamonds real diamonds?

**Yes — completely.** IGI, GCAL, and GIA all certify lab marquise on the same grading scale as natural stones. They are not simulants (cubic zirconia, moissanite) — they are structurally and chemically identical to mined diamonds. Any jeweller or gemologist examining the stone without disclosure cannot tell it apart from natural without a specialised detector.

### How much does a 1ct lab grown marquise diamond cost?

**$629–$958** for IGI E-D, VVS2-VVS1 certified stones. The entry is [IGI 1ct E-VVS2 Excellent at $629](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29389739?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). The sweet spot is [IGI 1ct D-VVS1 Ideal at $958](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29978545?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). A natural 1ct G-VS2 marquise costs ~$3,000 — the lab stone saves 79%.

### How much does a 2ct lab grown marquise diamond cost?

**$2,232–$5,291** in current certified inventory. The entry [IGI E-VVS2 is $2,232](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30118118?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). The GCAL D-IF ceiling is $5,291. Natural 2ct G-VS2 marquise starts near $32,000 — savings at the lab entry: 93%. See our [2 carat marquise diamond price guide](/2-carat-marquise-diamond-price).

### What is the GCAL 8X grade guarantee?

GCAL tests each diamond 8 times independently. If the stone grades higher on any of those 8 attempts than its certificate states, GCAL upgrades the report at no charge. This is the strongest grade-accuracy commitment in the industry — more rigorous than anything IGI or GIA offers. The premium is 15–25% over an equivalent IGI stone. Worth it at 1.5ct+ for anyone buying with heirloom intent.

### Is IGI certification reliable for lab marquise?

**Yes — IGI is the industry standard for lab diamonds.** Their grading scale matches GIA's for colour and clarity. IGI Ideal/Excellent cut grades screen effectively for bow-tie risk in marquise. Always verify the certificate number at igi.org before purchase. Over 85% of certified lab marquise inventory carries an IGI certificate.

### What is the bow-tie in a marquise diamond?

A bow-tie is a dark shadow across the belly of a marquise caused by light leakage from poor cut geometry. It affects lab and natural marquise equally — it's purely a cut quality issue. Selecting IGI Ideal or Excellent cut grades reduces the worst cases, but the only reliable test is watching the stone's 360° video. See our full [marquise diamond bow-tie guide](/marquise-diamond-bow-tie).

### Do lab grown marquise diamonds hold their value?

**No — resale value is low.** Lab diamond prices have fallen 70%+ since 2021 and continue to decline as reactor capacity scales. A $2,400 lab marquise may sell for $400–$700 in five years. Buy lab as a permanent stone you love wearing. Do not buy it as a store of value or investment.

### What L:W ratio should I target for a lab marquise?

**1.95–2.05** is the sweet spot. Below 1.85, the shape looks stubby. Above 2.10, the tips become fragile. With 313 lab marquise stones in inventory, you can reject anything outside 1.95–2.05 without penalty — no scarcity premium means no compromise on shape quality. See our [marquise diamond length to width ratio guide](/marquise-diamond-length-to-width-ratio).

### What colour grade is best for a lab marquise?

**E is the practical sweet spot for white metal settings.** D adds cost for an imperceptible upgrade. F is fine with no visible difference from E. In yellow or rose gold, G–H reads beautifully — the warm metal makes D-E colour redundant and saves $150–$300. See our [marquise diamond colour guide](/marquise-diamond-color-guide).

### What clarity grade should I pick for a lab marquise?

**VS2 minimum, VVS2 preferred.** VS2 is eye-clean at any marquise carat weight. VVS2 and VVS1 give a cleaner certificate and slightly better resale position. IF and FL add a premium for zero visual benefit — save that money for a larger stone. See our [marquise diamond clarity guide](/marquise-diamond-clarity-guide).

### CVD vs HPHT — which lab growing method is better?

**No difference in final quality.** CVD (Chemical Vapour Deposition) and HPHT (High Pressure High Temperature) both produce the same carbon crystal structure. The polished stone has no physical properties that differ by growth method. Certificates do not specify the growth method. Buy on grade, not on process.

### What setting works best with a lab grown marquise?

Any standard marquise setting works — lab stones have identical dimensions to natural at each carat weight. For tip protection: six-prong solitaire or bezel. For sparkle: petite pavé solitaire. For statement: three-stone with marquise flankers. Lab economics make three-stone and halo settings genuinely affordable at 2ct+. See the [marquise bezel engagement ring guide](/marquise-bezel-engagement-ring) and [marquise halo engagement ring guide](/marquise-halo-engagement-ring).

### Is a 3ct lab marquise a good engagement ring choice?

**Yes — 3ct lab marquise at $3,819 is exceptional value.** At 3ct, the marquise measures approximately 16x8mm face-up — a long, dramatic stone. Natural 3ct marquise starts at $65,000. Lab delivers the same diamond at 94% less. The considerations: secure setting (V-tip prongs or partial bezel), bow-tie screening via 360° video, and ideal 1.95–2.05 L:W. See the [4 carat marquise diamond price guide](/4-carat-marquise-diamond-price) if you want to go past 3ct.

### Where can I browse all certified lab grown marquise diamonds?

The [full certified lab marquise inventory](https://www.bluenile.com/lab-diamonds/marquise-cut-diamonds?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) has 313 stones from $454 to $11k+. Filter by carat, colour, clarity, and cut grade. Always watch the 360° stone video before purchasing — especially for bow-tie assessment on any marquise stone.

### How is a lab marquise different from a moissanite marquise?

**They are completely different materials.** A lab marquise diamond is pure carbon — chemically and physically identical to a mined diamond. A moissanite is silicon carbide — a different material with different optical properties (higher fire but lower hardness). Lab diamonds and moissanite are both alternatives to natural diamonds but serve different purposes and budgets. Lab diamond is the direct substitute for natural diamond.

## Final Verdict — Lab Grown Marquise for 4 Buyer Scenarios

### Scenario 1 — Best Value 1ct at $629 (ENTRY PICK)

**Buy the [IGI 1ct E-VVS2 Excellent at $629](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29389739?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).** Pair with a six-prong solitaire from $1,355. Total ring: under $2,100. You save $2,371 vs the natural G-VS2 equivalent. Certified, graded, 79% savings, and a higher actual grade than the natural comparison stone.

